@app.cell(hide_code=True)
def _():
    mo.md(r"""
    This notebook is acting as my submission for the Hands On Neuroscience assignment, but more than that I think it is a good opportunity to learn/practice cleaning EEG data!

    ## Reflection

    In class we had an EEG demonstration where a few people ( myself included! ) got to try on some research grade EEG headsets. I was especially excited to do this, as while I have a good deal of experience working with EEG data it had always been of a consumer calibre of equipment. I have written on this website about prior experiences with building my own EEG headset ( the [[Ultracortex Mark IV EEG Headset]] ), which in summary was a fun experience but ultimately a very humbling one as the data was essentially impossible to make use of. Even later on moving on to a prebuilt EEG headset with a much better polling rate ( the [[BrainAccess HALO]] ), the 4 channels it had were extremely limiting. This was a harsh but useful lesson for myself, but here I think we get to generalize this to all forms of brain signal sensing techniques.

    In class, instead of dealing with jank 3d printed aliexpress sourced equipment, we got to use the real deal. I can't remember exactly, but I think these headsets ( which were honestly more like nets over your face ) had somethink like 128 electrodes! It was quite the hastle to put on, I remember the period in which we were trying to figure out the approach to get this elastic net over my head. Even after we had gotten it over my large head ( I think the only reason I got selected for one of the experiments was because all the other volunteers were only candidates for the smaller headset ), there were still some clear issues. This was a "dry" electrode setup, which is the faster setup choice for EEG. However, even when using salt water in my hair to improve conductivity, we were struggling to get any meaningful signal from the array. This is likely do to my very thick hair, which for hte next few minutes I spent trying to wriggle electrodes through to get a solid grounding on my scalp. However even after all this time we were not even close; this is some 128 electrodes after all and it was taking at least a minute for each one. For the few sensors that worked, immediate visualization of the data gave the impression that we had created the worlds best everything detector, particularly useful for blinks! In the classic tradeoffs for F1 scoring, we could detect all events with 100% accuracy but also detect everything as an event.

    I reflect on this experience fondly, but ultimately a clear reminder of the limits of non-invasive measurement techniques. It is crazy to think that this was the "fast" setup, a gel based EEG headset, while having much clearer signal, would have likely taken 1-2 hours. The setup felt very fragile, and even if I had the time to fully set each of these electrodes I can imagine how easy it would be for one of them to get unseated during activities worth studying like sleep. Brain imageing/signal recording feels like a balance of 3 metrics: cost, practicality, and quality. fMRI is still noninvasive and great data, however cant really get these on amazon. EEG as discussed above is probably the cheapest entry point but lacking in accuracy, even when you scale up to the point of research level budgets. And even so it doesnt feel quite practical despite being championed as so. Sharp electrode recordings are probably more economically accessible than an fMRI machine and have the best data quality, but it is not practical for human trials at all.

    ## What does better EEG really afford us?

    That is most of my reflection, but the real point of this notebook was getting a proper sense for how this data is used so we can see in practice what makes the data from a gel based EEG headset better than a dry one, or how 19 electrodes compare to 4.

    I downloaded some internet data for various EEG headset, for investigating the fun task of "blink detector", or at least how much we can work with the data despite that. Each recording is 90 seconds of someone sitting still with their eyes opening and closing. We are hoping to recover the alpha swell (8-13 hz) that arises at the back of the head when someone closes their eyes. A perfectly clean recording should show far more alpha waves with the eyes closed.

    Choose the headset you want to start cleaning!
    """)
    return


